= 1. The Imperative for International Organisations

In an anarchic international system composed of sovereign nation-states, international organisations are not supranational world governments. They possess no sovereign authority over independent nations, nor do they command independent standing armies. Instead, they represent vital multilateral instruments:

== Facilitating Cooperation in an Interdependent World
- *The Philosophy of Multilateralism:* Former UN Secretary-General *Dag Hammarskjöld* famously captured the core philosophical justification of the world body: *"The United Nations was not created to take mankind to heaven, but to save humanity from hell."* International organisations provide regularized institutional mechanisms for dialogue, arbitration, and de-escalation, preventing misunderstandings from degenerating into catastrophic armed warfare.
- *Provision of Global Public Goods:* In the modern globalized epoch, sovereign states face existential collective action problems that no individual nation—regardless of military might or economic wealth—can resolve unilaterally:
  - *Transboundary Environmental Crises:* Anthropogenic climate change, ozone layer depletion, and global marine pollution require universally binding emissions regulations.
  - *Global Pandemics:* Controlling viral outbreaks (such as smallpox, polio, and COVID-19) requires coordinated real-time epidemiological surveillance, vaccine distribution, and technical assistance.
  - *International Communication and Transportation:* Global aviation, maritime navigation corridors, satellite communications, and cyberspace management depend fundamentally on standardized multilateral protocols.

== Sovereign Equality vs Structural Hierarchy
While the foundational principle of international law affirms the *sovereign equality* of all nation-states, the operational mechanics of international institutions invariably reflect real-world distributions of economic, military, and diplomatic power.

= 2. Evolution and Principal Organs of the United Nations

The United Nations was forged during the Second World War as the successor to the failed *League of Nations*, which had collapsed due to structural deficiencies and its inability to thwart fascist aggression in the 1930s:

== Historical Steps Leading to the San Francisco Charter
- *Atlantic Charter (August 1941):* Signed aboard the warship HMS Prince of Wales by US President Franklin D. Roosevelt and British Prime Minister Winston Churchill, establishing foundational principles for post-war peace.
- *Declaration by United Nations (January 1942):* Representatives of 26 Allied nations endorsed the Atlantic Charter, adopting the term "United Nations" coined by President Roosevelt.
- *Yalta Conference (February 1945):* The "Big Three" (Roosevelt, Churchill, and Joseph Stalin) finalized plans for the post-war security organisation and agreed upon the Security Council voting procedure.
- *San Francisco Conference (April–June 1945):* Delegates from 50 nations convened at the United Nations Conference on International Organization to draft the Charter of the United Nations, signed on *26 June 1945*. Poland, absent from the conference, signed later on 15 October 1945, bringing the total number of original founding members to 51.
- *Entry into Legal Force (24 October 1945):* The UN officially came into legal existence on *24 October 1945*, following ratification of the Charter by the P5 and a majority of signatories. *24 October* is celebrated globally as *United Nations Day*.
- *India's Accession:* India formally signed the UN Charter on *30 October 1945*, entering the organization as an original founding member state prior to achieving formal independence.
- *Universal Membership:* The UN expanded from its original 51 members to 193 sovereign states, with *South Sudan* admitted as the 193rd member state in *July 2011*.

