== The Six Principal Organs of the UN Architecture
1. *The General Assembly (UNGA):*
   - The primary deliberative, representative, and policymaking organ composed of all 193 member states.
   - Operates strictly on the principle of *"one state, one vote"*.
   - Decides vital questions—including recommendations on peace and security, election of non-permanent Security Council members, and approval of the UN budget—by a *two-thirds majority*. Other questions require a simple majority.
   - Resolutions passed by the General Assembly are politically influential but legally *non-binding* recommendations.
2. *The Security Council (UNSC):*
   - Entrusted under the UN Charter with primary responsibility for maintaining international peace and security.
   - Consists of *15 members*: *5 Permanent Members (P5)*—the United States, the United Kingdom, France, the Russian Federation (which inherited the Soviet seat in 1991), and the People's Republic of China (which replaced Taiwan in 1971)—and *10 Non-Permanent Members*.
   - Non-permanent members are elected by the General Assembly for staggered *two-year terms* according to regional geographical quotas. They *cannot be immediately re-elected* upon the completion of their two-year term.
   - *The Veto Power (Article 27):* Substantive decisions require 9 affirmative votes, including the concurring votes of all five permanent members. A single negative vote cast by any P5 member constitutes a *veto*, completely defeating the resolution even if supported by the remaining 14 members.
3. *The Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC):*
   - Comprises *54 member states* elected by the General Assembly for overlapping *three-year terms*.
   - Coordinates the international economic, social, cultural, educational, and health work of the UN system, serving as the central forum for discussing international development goals and supervising specialized agencies (such as WHO, UNESCO, ILO, and UNICEF).
4. *The International Court of Justice (ICJ):*
   - The principal judicial organ of the United Nations, seated permanently at the Peace Palace in *The Hague, Netherlands* (the only principal organ not located at UN Headquarters in New York).
   - Comprises *15 judges* elected for *nine-year terms* by simultaneous absolute majority votes in both the General Assembly and the Security Council.
   - Adjudicates legal disputes between sovereign states and delivers advisory opinions on legal questions referred by authorized UN organs and specialized agencies.
5. *The Secretariat & The Secretary-General:*
   - The administrative backbone of the UN, comprising international civil servants administering programs and policies.
   - Headed by the *Secretary-General*, the chief administrative officer appointed by the General Assembly upon the formal recommendation of the Security Council for a renewable *five-year term*.
   - The Secretary-General personifies the moral authority of the UN, utilizing "good offices" diplomacy to mediate international disputes.
6. *The Trusteeship Council:*
   - Established to supervise the administration of 11 trust territories placed under the International Trusteeship System to prepare them for self-governance or independence.
   - With the independence of *Palau* (the last remaining trust territory) on 1 October 1994, the Trusteeship Council formally *suspended its operations on 1 November 1994*, agreeing to meet only as occasion required.

